# Target C — audited §171 contact-chain safe area and constructive Type-I capacity

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only Type-I route theorem; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module normalizes the contact-wall, exterior mixed-wall, and contact-chain
safe-area results previously carried in `research/`. It sharpens §165: the replacement
packing need not lie in the full depth-one inner parallel body. Actual unit-contact
boundary edges are exact geometric shields, so along those walls it suffices to stay
one unit from the **boundary source vertices**.

The universal one-edge constant is

\[
\boxed{
c_*={\pi\over6}+{\sqrt3\over4},
\qquad
g_*:=1-c_*>0,
}
\tag{171.1}
\]

and

\[
h_*:={\pi\over2}-c_*={\pi\over3}-{\sqrt3\over4}.
\tag{171.2}
\]

The theorem gives explicit finite residual thresholds which, if met by one macroscopic
Type-I container, realize enough protected unit-packing slots to delete a vertex cover
of the complete actual diameter graph and force strict diameter descent.

---

## 171.1 Exact one-edge forbidden-area increment

Let `p=(0,0)`, `q=(1,0)`, and `H={y>=0}`. For the unit disks `B_p,B_q`,

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{area}((B_q\cap H)\setminus B_p)
=c_*.
}
\tag{171.3}
\]

Indeed a half unit disk has area `pi/2`, while the overlap lens of two unit disks with
centers one unit apart has full area

\[
{2\pi\over3}-{\sqrt3\over2}.
\]

The edge line bisects that lens, so the new one-sided area is

\[
{\pi\over2}-\left({\pi\over3}-{\sqrt3\over4}\right)
={\pi\over6}+{\sqrt3\over4}.
\]

Now let

\[
W=(p_0,p_1,\ldots,p_\ell)
\]

be any unit-contact path on the boundary of a convex polygon `C`. Put

\[
U_W=C\cap\bigcup_{i=0}^{\ell}\overline B(p_i,1).
\]

Convexity puts `C` in the interior half-plane of each preceding boundary edge. The
first disk contributes at most `pi/2`; for `i>=1`, the part of the `i`th disk not
already covered by all earlier disks is contained in the one-sided part not covered by
`B(p_{i-1},1)`, hence costs at most `c_*`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{area}(U_W)
\le {\pi\over2}+\ell c_*.
}
\tag{171.4}
\]

No small-turn or nonconsecutive-disjointness hypothesis is used; extra disk overlap only
improves the bound.

---

## 171.2 Unit contact edges are exact crossing shields

The geometric input is the following two-endpoint barrier.

Let `|p-q|=1`, `|x-p|,|x-q|>=1`, and `|x-y|<1`. If the open segment `xy` properly
crosses `pq`, then

\[
\boxed{|y-p|<1,
\qquad |y-q|<1.}
\tag{171.5}
\]

If `r` is the crossing point,

\[
|x-p|+|y-q|
\le |x-r|+|r-p|+|y-r|+|r-q|
=|x-y|+|p-q|<2,
\]

so `|y-q|<1`; interchange `p,q` for the other inequality.

### Internal face shield

Let `C` be a convex bounded contact face with cyclic boundary vertices
`p_1,...,p_m`, every boundary edge a unit contact. If `y in int(C)` satisfies

\[
|y-p_i|\ge1
\qquad(1\le i\le m),
\tag{171.6}
\]

then for **every** old source point `x outside int(C)`,

\[
\boxed{|x-y|\ge1.}
\tag{171.7}
\]

Otherwise the segment from `y` to `x` first exits through either a boundary vertex or
the interior of one unit boundary edge. A vertex crossing contradicts source
unit-separation plus (171.6); an edge-interior crossing contradicts (171.5).

Thus a simultaneous replacement set inside `C` needs only pairwise unit separation and
distance at least one from the boundary source vertices.

### Exterior mixed-wall shield

Let an exterior cap have boundary

\[
\partial C=[u,v]\cup W,
\qquad
W=(u=p_0,p_1,\ldots,p_\ell=v),
\tag{171.8}
\]

where `[u,v]` is the artificial force-hull closing chord and `W` is the complementary
unit-contact path. Define

\[
S_{mix}(C)=\{y\in int(C):
\operatorname{dist}(y,[u,v])\ge1,
\ |y-p_i|\ge1\ \forall i\}.
\tag{171.9}
\]

If `y in S_mix(C)` and `x` is any old source point outside `int(C)`, then

\[
\boxed{|x-y|\ge1.}
\tag{171.10}
\]

The first boundary crossing of a hypothetical segment of length `<1` is either on the
closing chord, contradicting the chord-distance guard, or on the actual contact path,
where (171.5) or the vertex guard applies.

Hence either Type-I geometry admits a replacement region protected against **all**
retained old source points, without eroding actual unit-contact walls by a full unit
collar.

---

## 171.3 Safe-area lower bounds

### Internal exceptional face

For a convex internal contact face with `m` boundary unit edges, define

\[
S_\partial(C)=\{y\in int(C): |y-p_i|\ge1\ \forall i\}.
\]

Open the boundary cycle into a path through all `m` vertices and use (171.4) on its
`m-1` unit edges. The union of boundary-vertex forbidden disks inside `C` has area at
most

\[
{\pi\over2}+(m-1)c_*=mc_*+h_*.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{area}S_\partial(C)
\ge A_C-mc_*-h_*.
}
\tag{171.11}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\operatorname{area}S_\partial(C)
\ge A_C-m+g_*m-h_*.
\tag{171.12}
\]

### Exterior cap

Let the closing chord have length `s=|u-v|` and let the contact path contain `ell`
unit edges. The one-sided unit neighborhood of the chord has area `s+pi/2`; inside
`C` it already covers the endpoint-disk constraints at `u,v`. Add only the
`ell-1` internal path-vertex disks. By (171.3) each adds at most `c_*`, so

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{area}S_{mix}(C)
\ge A_C-s-\ell c_*-h_*.
}
\tag{171.13}
\]

Since `per(C)=s+ell`,

\[
\operatorname{area}S_{mix}(C)
\ge A_C-\operatorname{per}(C)+g_*\ell-h_*.
\tag{171.14}
\]

The gain `g_* ell` is the exact universal linear area recovered by replacing a full
contact-side collar with endpoint guards.

---

## 171.4 Constructive lattice realization

Let `Lambda` be the unit triangular lattice with covolume

\[
a_0={\sqrt3\over2}.
\]

Translation averaging gives, for every bounded measurable safe region `S`,

\[
\boxed{
\max_t |(\Lambda+t)\cap S|
\ge\left\lfloor{\operatorname{area}S\over a_0}\right\rfloor.
}
\tag{171.15}
\]

The selected lattice points are pairwise unit-separated; the shields above give unit
separation from every retained old source point. This is an actual replacement packing,
not a density heuristic or reverse Oler step.

Let `H` be the **complete actual diameter graph** and `tau(H)` its minimum vertex-cover
number. Deleting a cover `U`, deleting the assigned chamber isolates, and inserting
`z_C+|U|` safe lattice points gives the same strict-diameter contradiction as §165.
Thus enough safe-region capacity closes the corresponding Type-I branch directly.

---

## 171.5 Internal residual threshold

For an internal contact face define the exact boundary residual

\[
\boxed{
r_\partial
={A_C\over a_0}-{m\over2}+1-z_C.
}
\tag{171.16}
\]

If `sigma_C=A_C/a_0+m/2+1-z_C` is the isolate-only chamber Oler slack, then

\[
\boxed{r_\partial=\sigma_C-m\ge0.}
\tag{171.17}
\]

The inequality follows by applying finite Oler to the full unit-separated face set
consisting of all `m` boundary vertices plus the `z_C` assigned isolates. Thus the
prescribed unit boundary itself consumes exactly `m` units of the coarse chamber slack.

Put

\[
\kappa_*={\pi\over3\sqrt3},
\qquad
C_*={1\over2}+{2\pi\over3\sqrt3}.
\tag{171.18}
\]

Since

\[
{c_*\over a_0}={1\over2}+\kappa_*,
\qquad
{h_*\over a_0}=2\kappa_*-{1\over2},
\]

(171.11) and lattice averaging give

\[
\boxed{
M_\partial(C)-z_C
\ge
\left\lfloor r_\partial-\kappa_*m-C_*\right\rfloor.
}
\tag{171.19}
\]

Hence the internal Type-I branch is eliminated whenever

\[
\boxed{
r_\partial
\ge\tau(H)+\kappa_*m+C_*.
}
\tag{171.20}
\]

This is a finite constructive sufficient condition.

---

## 171.6 Exterior residual threshold

For an exterior cap let `k_C=ell+1` be its augmented graph-boundary vertex count and
put

\[
\sigma_C={A_C\over a_0}+{s+\ell\over2}+1-z_C,
\qquad
r_C:=\sigma_C-k_C.
\tag{171.21}
\]

Then

\[
{A_C\over a_0}=z_C+r_C+{\ell\over2}-{s\over2}.
\tag{171.22}
\]

Set

\[
\lambda_*={2\over\sqrt3}+{1\over2}.
\tag{171.23}
\]

Using (171.13) and lattice averaging,

\[
\boxed{
M_{mix}(C)-z_C
\ge
\left\lfloor
r_C-\kappa_*\ell-\lambda_*s-{h_*\over a_0}
\right\rfloor.
}
\tag{171.24}
\]

Therefore the exterior Type-I branch is eliminated whenever

\[
\boxed{
r_C
\ge
\tau(H)+\kappa_*\ell+\lambda_*s+{h_*\over a_0}.
}
\tag{171.25}
\]

The artificial closing chord is more expensive than a unit-contact side because it has
no unit-edge crossing shield.

---

## 171.7 Frontier

The companion residual-conservation identity writes the sum of chamber residuals as one
punctured force-hull residual. Section §171 does **not** yet force a selected macroscopic
container above (171.20) or (171.25); that is now the exact remaining Type-I capacity
problem.

The improvement over §165 is nevertheless concrete:

- the geometric protection region is strictly larger than `C_{-1}`;
- the gain along each contact-wall edge is the exact positive constant `g_*`;
- Oler slack is converted to realized unit-packing slots by translation averaging;
- the remaining deficit is an explicit linear residual inequality, not a qualitative
  “collective exchange” obligation.

## Scope audit

- The contact-wall shield uses only convexity, source unit separation, and actual unit
  contact boundary edges.
- The exterior theorem guards exactly the artificial chord geometrically and the actual
  contact path by endpoint disks.
- Every deleted source point is replaced; the direct competitor need not be
  triangle-free.
- The diameter cover is taken in the complete actual diameter graph, not a selected
  positive KKT support.
- Floors are retained; the closure thresholds are finite.
- No Oler upper bound is reversed into a capacity lower bound: realization comes from
  triangular-lattice translation averaging.
- No contact isolate enters the positive-core affine kernel.

**Independent audit disposition:** PASS at audited-working proof-only scope. C-II and
Level I remain open.
